<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Trapezus</h1><p class="st-meta">756 BCE – 63 BCE (approx.) · capital Trapezus</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Trapezus, a Greek colony on the south-eastern Black Sea coast that became Trebizond, where Xenophon's Ten Thousand reached the sea.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Colonists from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sinope">Sinope</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trapezus</a> on a table-shaped headland, from which its name comes, traditionally in 756 BCE. It controlled a harbour and the route inland to the Armenian highlands, among the Colchian and Pontic peoples of the coast.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trapezus</a> was a Greek city governed by its citizens, paying tribute to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sinope">Sinope</a> and later recognising Persian overlordship. In 400 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-thousand">Ten Thousand</a> Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> arrived here after their march, and the city hosted them for a month. It traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, metals and hazelnuts.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trapezus</a> was absorbed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus</a> in the 2nd century BCE and passed to the Roman world after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI</a> in 63 BCE. It later became <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Trebizond</a>, capital of the Empire of Trebizond.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/trapezus.jpg" alt="Trapezus" width="320" height="239"><figcaption>Image: Francesco Bini · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trebisonda,_ex-chiesa_della_panagia_Chrysokephalos,_oggi_moschea_fatih,_esterno_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>756 BCE – 63 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Trapezus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Trebizond (ancient)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-410&amp;focus=trapezus" aria-label="Trapezus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5652 714 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Trapezus lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5972 970L5854 938M5972 970L5879 982"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5854 938h0M5879 982h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5972 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5972 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-410&amp;focus=trapezus">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

